/*
  Psychiatric Interviewing Trainer — Part III content (Chapters 16–18)
  Mastering Complex Interviewing Tasks Demanded in Everyday Clinical Practice
  Based on Shea, Psychiatric Interviewing: The Art of Understanding (3rd ed.).
  Written in our own words. All clinical examples are composed for teaching; they are not transcripts from the book.

  Same schema as part1-chapters.js. Chapters appear in the app as they are added;
  Chapters still listed in UPCOMING (app.js) show as "Coming soon" until then.
*/
window.TRAINER = window.TRAINER || { parts: {} };
(function () {
  var part = (window.TRAINER.parts.part3 = window.TRAINER.parts.part3 || {});
  part.id = "part3";
  part.numeral = "III";
  part.title = "Mastering Complex Interviewing Tasks Demanded in Everyday Clinical Practice";
  part.blurb = "The mental status, and the sensitive exploration of suicidal, violent, and homicidal ideation.";
  part.simulations = part.simulations || [];
  part.drills = part.drills || [];

  part.chapters = [
    /* ───────────────────────── CHAPTER 16 ───────────────────────── */
    {
      id: "ch16", num: 16, title: "The Mental Status", sub: "How to perform and document it effectively",
      summary: "The mental status is an objective snapshot of the patient during the interview itself. Most of it is woven invisibly into the conversation; formal cognitive testing is added when the picture calls for it. Write what you saw and what the patient said, in precise shared terms, with pertinent negatives, and save your opinions for the formulation.",
      concepts: [
        { h: "Empathy can fog the lens", b: "Looking with the patient is our natural stance, but leaning on it alone can hide what is in front of us, such as a subtle loosening of associations. The mental status asks you to step back far enough to observe with objective compassion." },
        { h: "Impact status vs mental status", b: "The impact status is your moment-to-moment read of the patient's affect and behavior, including how you are affecting them. The mental status is the composite of everything observed across the interview: a snapshot of the session, set against the ongoing story told in the HPI." },
        { h: "Two parts, one mostly invisible", b: "Most of the mental status is the symptom review and observation woven through the interview with good facilics, so the patient never feels examined. The second part, formal cognitive testing (orientation, digit spans, recall, drawings), is a distinct, optional section expanded by age and suspicion of delirium or dementia." },
        { h: "Facts here, opinions in the formulation", b: "The HPI and mental status record facts: the patient's words, behaviors, and symptoms. Diagnoses, interpretations, and treatment plans belong in the Narrative Summary and Clinical Formulation. The mental status covers only what this patient showed and said today; don't repeat the HPI, refer to it." },
        { h: "Six headings", b: "Appearance and behavior; speech characteristics and thought process; thought content; perception; mood and affect; sensorium, cognitive functioning, and insight. Checklists can flag pathology, but a checklist alone is never an adequate mental status." },
        { h: "Process vs content", b: "\"Thought disorder\" is ambiguous. Problems in how thoughts are formed (derailment, tangentiality, illogic) are a formal thought disorder and go under thought process. Problems in what is believed are delusions and go under thought content. A patient can be floridly psychotic with perfectly formed sentences." },
        { h: "Ruminations, obsessions, compulsions", b: "Ruminations are absorbing worries that don't feel foreign. Obsessions are intrusive, unwanted, and feel odd and painful; the patient tries to stop them. Compulsions are acts or mental acts the patient feels driven to repeat, often to relieve an obsession's anxiety. Ask every patient." },
        { h: "Mood is told, affect is seen", b: "Mood is the patient's report of how they have been feeling, and is relatively lasting. Affect is what you observe in the room, and can change minute to minute. If the patient won't describe mood, say so; you can always describe affect. Name the affect first, then judge its appropriateness." },
        { h: "Consciousness first", b: "Always document level of consciousness. A fluctuating sensorium raises delirium, a potentially lethal emergency. Attention-heavy bedside tests (digit spans, vigilance, clock, cube) are sensitive to it; the MoCA and CAM help structure the search." }
      ],
      pearls: [
        "Describe, don't label: write what the clothes, hands, and movements looked like, not \"odd\" or \"inappropriate.\"",
        "Quote the patient. Their exact words show conviction better than \"clearly delusional.\"",
        "Grade severity: mild, moderate, or severe tangentiality or derailment.",
        "Pertinent negatives show you looked: \"no evidence of responding to internal stimuli.\"",
        "Write \"denied hallucinations,\" not \"no hallucinations.\"",
        "Suicide, violence, and self-harm get a line in every mental status: ideation, plan, intent, and any action taken.",
        "State the affect, then whether it fits the content.",
        "Record exactly which cognitive tests you did, and say when you did none."
      ],
      practice: [],
      quiz: [
        { q: "What best distinguishes the impact status from the mental status?", opts: ["The impact status is formal cognitive testing; the mental status is observation", "The impact status is a moment-to-moment read of the patient's affect and behavior; the mental status is the composite of observations across the interview", "The impact status is recorded in the HPI; the mental status in the formulation", "They are two names for the same thing"], a: 1, w: "The impact status is the quick, subjective take at any single moment (including your own effect on the patient). The mental status is the objective snapshot of the whole interview." },
        { q: "Which statement belongs in the Narrative Summary and Clinical Formulation rather than the mental status?", opts: ["\"States, 'The cameras in the ceiling are for me.'\"", "\"Clothes stained; hair uncombed; picked at her sleeve throughout.\"", "\"Presentation most consistent with a manic episode with psychotic features.\"", "\"Denied current suicidal ideation, plan, or intent.\""], a: 2, w: "A diagnostic impression is an opinion about what the facts mean. The mental status records the facts themselves." },
        { q: "A patient is grossly delusional and agitated but speaks in clear, logically connected sentences. How should thought process be described?", opts: ["Formal thought disorder, because the patient is psychotic", "Without derailment, tangentiality, or illogic; the delusions are documented under thought content", "Content thought disorder under thought process", "Flight of ideas"], a: 1, w: "\"Psychotic\" is not the same as \"formal thought disorder.\" Formal thought disorder concerns how thoughts are formed; delusions are thought content." },
        { q: "A patient wanders from topic to topic and eventually returns to answer the original question. This is:", opts: ["Tangential thought", "Circumstantial thought", "Derailment", "Thought blocking"], a: 1, w: "Circumstantial thought takes the long way around but gets back. Tangential thought wanders off and never returns." },
        { q: "Pressured speech is best described as:", opts: ["Speech that is simply fast", "Speech that is loud", "Speech without the normal pauses, hard to interrupt, which can occur even at a normal rate", "Speech with long latencies before answers"], a: 2, w: "Pressure is the loss of natural pauses, \"speech without punctuation.\" It usually accompanies a fast rate but is not the same thing." },
        { q: "A patient stops mid-sentence, looks distracted, and never returns to the thought. Which term fits?", opts: ["Thought blocking", "Illogical thought", "Long response latency", "Circumstantiality"], a: 0, w: "Thought blocking is often a sign of psychosis, as when a hallucination interrupts. It is different from a long silence before answering." },
        { q: "Which feature most distinguishes an obsession from a rumination?", opts: ["Obsessions are about the past", "Obsessions feel intrusive, odd, and painful, and the patient tries to stop them", "Ruminations are always about guilt", "Ruminations always lead to compulsions"], a: 1, w: "Ruminating patients are absorbed in their worries but don't experience the process as foreign. Obsessions feel alien and the patient fights them." },
        { q: "A woman looking at a coat on a hook suddenly sees it as a crouching figure. This is:", opts: ["A visual hallucination", "A visual illusion", "A delusion", "Derealization"], a: 1, w: "Illusions distort a real stimulus. Hallucinations arise without any external trigger, as in seeing a figure in an empty hallway." },
        { q: "A patient cries through much of the interview but says, \"My mood is great.\" How should the mental status read?", opts: ["\"Mood: depressed.\"", "\"Mood: euthymic. Affect: euthymic.\"", "\"Mood: 'great' (patient's words). Affect: sad, tearful at several points.\"", "Leave mood blank"], a: 2, w: "Record what the patient reports and what you observe. The discrepancy, and its meaning (perhaps denial), is discussed in the formulation." },
        { q: "Which screening tool was developed specifically to detect delirium?", opts: ["MMSE", "MoCA", "Confusion Assessment Method (CAM)", "Digit span alone"], a: 2, w: "The CAM was built for delirium. The MMSE and MoCA screen broader cognitive impairment, though Shea finds the MoCA better than the MMSE at catching delirium." }
      ],
      glossary: [
        { term: "Impact status", def: "The clinician's moment-to-moment read of the patient's immediate affect and behavior, including the effect the clinician's own behavior is having." },
        { term: "Mental status", def: "An objective description of the patient's appearance, behavior, thought, feeling, and perception during the interview itself, documented under six standard headings." },
        { term: "Informal cognitive examination", def: "Gauging orientation, attention, and memory by how the patient handles ordinary questions throughout the interview, without formal tests." },
        { term: "Formal cognitive examination", def: "A distinct section of testing (orientation, digit spans, recall, constructions, or a standardized battery), expanded according to the patient's age and suspected delirium or dementia." },
        { term: "Pertinent negative", def: "An abnormality you looked for and did not find, recorded to show the reader it was examined (e.g., no evidence of responding to internal stimuli)." },
        { term: "Narrative Summary and Clinical Formulation", def: "The section of the record where the clinician integrates history and mental status and gives opinions: diagnoses, etiology, and treatment plans." },
        { term: "Formal thought disorder", def: "A disturbance in how thoughts are formed and connected (derailment, severe tangentiality, illogic), documented under thought process." },
        { term: "Content thought disorder", def: "An older term for disturbed thought content, essentially delusions. Shea suggests avoiding the ambiguous \"thought disorder\" family of terms." },
        { term: "Pressured speech", def: "Speech without the normal pauses, hard to interrupt or get a word into. Usually fast, but can occur at a normal rate." },
        { term: "Tangential thought", def: "Thoughts drift off on tangents from the patient's own statements, with some connection between steps, and never return to the original point." },
        { term: "Circumstantial thought", def: "Thoughts take detours like tangential thought but eventually come back to the original topic." },
        { term: "Derailment", def: "The DSM-5 term for loosening of associations: successive thoughts seem unconnected to the listener. Moderate to severe degrees indicate psychosis unless feigned." },
        { term: "Word salad", def: "Derailment so severe that speech is essentially incoherent." },
        { term: "Flight of ideas", def: "Tangential thought or loosening of associations combined with marked pressure and usually rapid speech; often driven by distractions or plays on words. Typical of mania." },
        { term: "Thought blocking", def: "The patient stops mid-sentence and never returns to the idea, as if interrupted, often by a hallucination. Frequently a sign of psychosis." },
        { term: "Illogical thought", def: "Conclusions that don't follow from the premises. Distinct from a delusion, which is a false belief that may be reasoned quite logically." },
        { term: "Rumination", def: "Persistent, absorbing preoccupation with worries or guilt that does not feel foreign to the patient; common in depression and anxiety." },
        { term: "Obsession", def: "A recurrent, intrusive thought, image, or impulse that feels odd and distressing and that the patient tries to suppress." },
        { term: "Compulsion", def: "A behavior or mental act the patient feels driven to repeat, often to relieve the anxiety of an obsession, though recognized as excessive." },
        { term: "Reassurance compulsion", def: "A compulsion to ask others the same question repeatedly to quiet an obsession, such as asking a partner again and again whether one looks all right." },
        { term: "Mood", def: "The patient's own report of how they have been feeling; a symptom, relatively persistent." },
        { term: "Affect", def: "The emotional state the clinician observes in the interview through face, voice, and body; a sign, often transient." },
        { term: "Restricted affect", def: "Some decrease in the range and intensity of facial expression." },
        { term: "Blunted affect", def: "A fairly striking decrease in facial animation and emotional expression." },
        { term: "Flat affect", def: "Essentially no spontaneous facial expression." },
        { term: "Labile affect", def: "Rapid, often abrupt shifts in expressed emotion." },
        { term: "Level of consciousness", def: "Alertness and its stability: alert, hyper-alert, somnolent, or fluctuating. Fluctuation raises concern for delirium." },
        { term: "Digit span", def: "Repeating strings of digits forward and backward; a quick test of attention. Record the longest string managed in each direction." },
        { term: "Vigilance test", def: "The patient taps each time a target letter is heard in a string of random letters; sensitive to the inattention of delirium." },
        { term: "MMSE", def: "Folstein Mini-Mental State Examination: a 30-point standardized battery (orientation, registration, attention and calculation, recall, language, following commands). Its form requires a paid license." },
        { term: "MoCA", def: "Montreal Cognitive Assessment: a free one-page screening battery available in many languages. Shea finds it more efficient than the MMSE for detecting delirium." },
        { term: "CAM", def: "Confusion Assessment Method: a tool designed specifically to detect delirium." }
      ]
    },
    /* ───────────────────────── CHAPTER 17 ───────────────────────── */
    {
      id: "ch17", num: 17, title: "Exploring Suicidal Ideation", sub: "The delicate art of suicide assessment",
      summary: "A suicide assessment is three tasks: gather risk factors, warning signs, and protective factors; elicit the patient's suicidal ideation, planning, behaviors, and intent; and form a clinical judgment of foreseeable risk from both databases. Risk factors do not predict a given patient's imminent behavior. What the patient has actually thought, planned, rehearsed, and procured, drawn out with the CASE Approach, reflects intent far better than what the patient states.",
      concepts: [
        { h: "Three tasks, three skill sets", b: "Collect risk factors, warning signs, and protective factors (data gathering); elicit suicidal ideation, planning, behavior, desire, and intent (data gathering); then formulate risk (a cognitive and intuitive act). The CASE Approach handles only the second task and says nothing about the other two." },
        { h: "Risk factors are not predictors", b: "A risk factor describes a population; a warning sign belongs to this living patient. No collection of either has been shown to predict an imminent attempt. A woman with one point on a checklist can be at extreme risk if that point is a command hallucination. Risk factors tell you to be more tenacious, consider corroborative sources, and ask specific lines of questions." },
        { h: "Static, dynamic, protective", b: "Static factors (sex, age, prior attempts, a death that cannot be undone) cannot change. Dynamic factors (substance use, psychosis, depression, unemployment) are the signposts for matrix treatment planning. Protective factors, personal (reasons for living, fear of death, sobriety, impulse control) and external (family, restricted access to means, duties to others, pets), mitigate risk and should be sought deliberately." },
        { h: "Three dangerous psychotic processes", b: "Command hallucinations, feelings of alien control, and hyper-religious preoccupation with proving one's love for God or obeying a violent scripture. Ask every psychotic patient about alien control. With hyper-religious patients do not name the verse; ask which parts of scripture feel important, then whether any feel like a direction to do something, even to harm themselves. Many suicides in schizophrenia happen in a demoralized, relatively non-psychotic phase." },
        { h: "Suicide is interpersonal", b: "Joiner's interpersonal theory: thwarted belongingness plus perceived burdensomeness, made lethal by an acquired capability for suicide (familiarity with pain and death, past attempts, means in hand). Ask whether the patient feels connected, lives alone, has someone to call, and whether others would be 'better off.' Assess the environment too: a hostile home, a family lax about removing a gun, or a frank death wish from a relative raises risk." },
        { h: "Depression, anxiety, hopelessness", b: "Up to 90% of suicides occur in people with a psychiatric disorder, mood disorders first; 'do a good diagnostic assessment' is one of the best predictive tips. Panic or intense anxiety on top of depression triples the rate. Beck's work suggests hopelessness may matter more than depressive severity; feeling trapped and purposeless are the phenomenological drivers named in IS PATH WARM." },
        { h: "Chronic risk vs immediate risk", b: "Many risk factors over a long time mean chronic risk, which can often be managed as an outpatient. Few risk factors do not mean safety if a critical one is present. The tetrad of lethality flags foreseeable near-term risk and usually hospitalization: a recent serious attempt; acutely dangerous psychosis; serious intent relayed in the interview or shown in recent behavior; and the patient who flatly denies ideation because they intend to die and do not want to be stopped." },
        { h: "The equation of suicidal intent", b: "Real intent = stated intent + reflected intent + withheld intent. Reflected intent is the amount of thinking, planning, rehearsing, and procuring the patient has done; like stages of change in motivational theory, actions speak louder than words. The more a patient wants to die, the more they withhold, often sharing a minor method while hiding the method of choice." },
        { h: "Timing: the elicitation triad", b: "Raise suicide when engagement is sound, the patient is emotionally involved in the pain (depression, hopelessness, and anxiety; situational turmoil and anger; or psychotic process), and ideally after the patient hints. Asking too early loses the truth and sometimes the alliance. If a patient opens with suicide, acknowledge it, build engagement, then return by a referred gate." },
        { h: "The CASE Approach", b: "Four chronological regions, each explored fully before the next: presenting events (hours to weeks), recent events (the past 2 months), past events (earlier than 2 months), and immediate events (now and next). Validity techniques are woven in: shame attenuation or normalization to open, behavioral incidents to build a verbal video, gentle assumption for other methods, denial of the specific when you suspect withholding, the catch-all question, and symptom amplification for time spent thinking." }
      ],
      pearls: [
        "Ask with the words 'killing yourself' or 'taking your life,' not 'hurting yourself' (which invites answers about non-lethal self-harm) and not 'commit,' which smells of crime and sin.",
        "Never use a negative statement of inquiry ('You haven't thought of killing yourself?'). It asks for no, and patients try to please. Do not accept the first no: ask a second time, softer, for fleeting thoughts.",
        "You cannot plant the idea of suicide. Raising it lifts a burden and shows that help is a sentence away.",
        "Put the pen down. Note taking and reading questions off a screen hide the nonverbal leakage that tells you a plan is being withheld, and can inflame a paranoid patient.",
        "Four-step kick-start for any new method: Have you ever had it out while thinking of killing yourself? When was the last time? Where were you? Then a summarizing invitation to walk you through it step by step.",
        "When the video skips (a Nixon gap), rewind: go back to where the gap began and ask what happened next until it fills.",
        "Ask denials of the specific one at a time, with a pause for each answer. A cannon question lets the patient lie once; separate questions give you several chances to see the truth leaking.",
        "In the region of past events, gather only what changes today's triage: the most serious attempt, whether current triggers and state resemble it, the rough count of attempts, and the most recent one outside the 2-month window.",
        "Gatekeepers should document reflected intent in full before transport. The transportation cure is real: patients clam up by the time they reach the ED.",
        "Safety planning is usually the better deterrent. Safety contracting earns its place as an assessment tool: the interpersonal push of 'Can you really promise me?' can expose hesitation that changes a disposition. Neither guarantees safety."
      ],
      practice: [],
      quiz: [
        { q: "A patient scores one point on the SAD PERSONS scale. Which single finding would still place her at very high immediate risk?", opts: ["She is recently separated", "She has a psychotic process with command hallucinations to kill herself", "She has rheumatoid arthritis", "She drinks socially"], a: 1, w: "Risk factor checklists have no predictive power as a sum. A single critical warning sign, such as dangerous psychosis, can outweigh an otherwise low score. Risk factors prompt tenacity and specific questions; they do not decide dangerousness." },
        { q: "Which best states the difference between a risk factor and a warning sign?", opts: ["A risk factor is dynamic; a warning sign is static", "A risk factor describes a population of people who died by suicide; a warning sign is a characteristic of this living patient", "A warning sign is found only on the mental status", "There is no difference"], a: 1, w: "Risk factors are statistical associations in large samples. Warning signs (risk predictors) belong to the individual in front of you. Some items, such as intoxication and psychosis, are both." },
        { q: "Which pair of interpersonal experiences does Joiner's theory single out as especially dangerous when combined?", opts: ["Anger and shame", "Thwarted belongingness and perceived burdensomeness", "Grief and guilt", "Hopelessness and insomnia"], a: 1, w: "Joiner: feeling that one does not belong, plus believing one is a burden to others, becomes lethal when the person has acquired the capability to act (familiarity with pain and death, prior attempts, means at hand)." },
        { q: "A psychotic patient is preoccupied with a scripture verse about cutting off an offending hand. The safest way to explore this is to:", opts: ["Quote the verse and ask whether he intends to act on it", "Avoid religion entirely", "Ask which parts of scripture feel important to him, then whether any feel like a direction to do something, even to harm himself", "Ask his pastor instead"], a: 2, w: "Naming the verse risks being woven into the psychosis later as a message from God. Two open questions in tandem usually let a preoccupied patient reveal it." },
        { q: "Which of the following is NOT part of the tetrad of lethality?", opts: ["A recent serious suicide attempt", "A high total score on a risk factor scale", "Serious suicidal intent relayed in the interview or shown in recent behavior", "Flat denial of ideation by a patient who intends to die and does not want to be stopped"], a: 1, w: "The tetrad: recent serious attempt, acutely dangerous psychotic process, serious intent relayed or enacted, and the withholding patient. A high checklist total is not one of them." },
        { q: "The equation of suicidal intent says real intent equals:", opts: ["Stated intent only", "Stated intent plus reflected intent plus withheld intent", "Risk factors minus protective factors", "Hopelessness times depression"], a: 1, w: "Reflected intent (how much thinking, planning, rehearsing, and procuring has occurred) and withheld intent (what the patient will not say) matter as much as or more than what the patient states." },
        { q: "According to the elicitation of suicidal ideation triad, the best moment to raise suicide is when:", opts: ["The interview is just beginning, to get it out of the way", "Engagement is sound, the patient is emotionally involved in their pain, and ideally has hinted at the topic", "The patient has calmed down completely", "A family member is present"], a: 1, w: "Intense affect lowers defenses; sound engagement makes sharing safe; a hint means the door is already open. Asking too early is the most common timing error." },
        { q: "In the CASE Approach, which validity technique is used right after the first method has been fully explored, to look for other methods?", opts: ["Symptom amplification", "Denial of the specific", "Gentle assumption ('What other ways have you thought of killing yourself?')", "The catch-all question"], a: 2, w: "Gentle assumption searches for other methods, with a verbal video for each one found. Denials of the specific come only if you suspect withholding, then the catch-all question, then symptom amplification for time spent on the thoughts." },
        { q: "A patient says he took 'a handful' of pills and 'ended up in the emergency room.' What should the interviewer do?", opts: ["Accept the account and move to risk factors", "Clarify the number with a behavioral incident, then rewind to find out how he got to the ER", "Ask whether he was really trying to die", "Begin safety planning immediately"], a: 1, w: "'A handful' needs a number. The jump to the ER is a Nixon gap: rewind the verbal video with sequencing behavioral incidents. Whether he called someone himself is a lethal point that reflects intent." },
        { q: "Shea's main argument for safety contracting, used selectively, is that it:", opts: ["Reliably prevents attempts", "Replaces the need for hospitalization", "Works as an assessment tool: the interpersonal push can expose nonverbal leakage of ambivalence or intent", "Should be done at the start of every interview"], a: 2, w: "As a deterrent it guarantees nothing and often loses to safety planning. Asked earnestly, eye to eye, after a full assessment and never with borderline or passive-aggressive pathology, hesitation to promise can be the only sign of true immediate risk." }
      ],
      glossary: [
        { term: "Risk factor", def: "A characteristic found more often than expected in large samples of people who died by suicide: demographics, circumstances, history, clinical condition. Describes a population, not a person." },
        { term: "Warning sign", def: "A characteristic of this specific living patient that suggests increased likelihood of an attempt; the newer name for a risk predictor. Intoxication and psychosis are both risk factors and warning signs." },
        { term: "Static risk factor", def: "A risk factor that cannot be changed: sex, age, prior attempts, an irreversible loss." },
        { term: "Dynamic risk factor", def: "A risk factor that can be altered, such as substance use, psychosis, depression, or unemployment; each is a potential point of collaborative, matrix-wide intervention." },
        { term: "Protective factor", def: "An attribute or situation that may reduce the likelihood of an attempt. Personal (beliefs against suicide, hope, sobriety, impulse control, reasons for living, fear of death) or external (strong relationships, restricted access to means, duties to others, pets)." },
        { term: "Alien control", def: "The psychotic feeling of being controlled by an outside agent (a demon, a device, a signal, an online antagonist). Dangerous when the agent presses for suicide or homicide; ask every psychotic patient about it." },
        { term: "Abraham syndrome", def: "Hyper-religious preoccupation with the idea that God is testing the patient and wants an act of suicide, homicide, or self-mutilation as proof of love, sometimes with command hallucinations." },
        { term: "Interpersonal theory of suicide", def: "Joiner: suicidal desire arises from thwarted belongingness plus perceived burdensomeness; it becomes lethal when the person has acquired the capability to act." },
        { term: "Thwarted belongingness", def: "Joiner: the painful sense of not being connected to or part of a group. Probe: Do you feel connected to others? Do you live alone? Is there someone you can call?" },
        { term: "Acquired capability for suicide", def: "Joiner: reduced fear of pain and death gained through exposure (prior attempts, chronic illness, occupations around death), which makes it possible to act on suicidal desire." },
        { term: "SAD PERSONS", def: "Checklist of largely static risk factors: sex, age, depression, previous attempt, ethanol abuse, rational thinking loss, social supports lacking, organized plan, no spouse, sickness. Useful as a reminder; its total has no predictive value." },
        { term: "NO HOPE", def: "Shea's acronym of dynamic risk factors: no framework for meaning, overt change in clinical condition, hostile interpersonal environment, out of hospital recently, predisposing personality factors, excuses for dying strongly believed. The name itself prompts asking about hopelessness." },
        { term: "IS PATH WARM", def: "American Association of Suicidology mnemonic of dynamic warning signs: ideation, substance abuse, purposelessness, anger, trapped, hopelessness, withdrawing, anxiety, recklessness, mood change." },
        { term: "Tetrad of lethality", def: "Four situations suggesting foreseeable near-term risk and usually hospitalization: a recent serious attempt; acutely dangerous psychotic process; serious intent relayed in the interview or shown in recent behavior; flat denial by a patient who intends to die and does not want to be stopped." },
        { term: "Chronic suicide risk", def: "Risk built from many risk factors present over a long time; serious, but usually manageable as an outpatient with careful follow-up, unlike foreseeable immediate risk." },
        { term: "Foreseeable risk", def: "The clinically and medico-legally reasonable standard: estimating whether a specific patient is at increased risk in the near future. Clinicians cannot truly predict whether or when an attempt will occur." },
        { term: "Corroborative source", def: "A family member, friend, or other informant interviewed about statements, behaviors, weapons, and prior attempts. Safety concerns outweigh confidentiality; consult a colleague and document the reasoning." },
        { term: "Equation of suicidal intent", def: "Real suicidal intent = stated intent + reflected intent + withheld intent. The more a patient wants to die, the more is withheld." },
        { term: "Reflected intent", def: "The quality and quantity of suicidal thought, planning, rehearsal, and procurement of means, which reflects how much the patient truly wants to die, often more accurately than stated intent." },
        { term: "Withheld intent", def: "Intent the patient keeps back, consciously (to avoid being stopped or losing the method of choice) or unconsciously (defenses that make suicide feel like weakness or sin)." },
        { term: "Transportation cure", def: "The common pattern in which a patient who disclosed suicidal ideation to a gatekeeper clams up by the time they reach the emergency department; a reason to document reflected intent in full before referral." },
        { term: "Elicitation of suicidal ideation triad", def: "The best time to raise suicide: sound engagement, intense emotional involvement in the pain, and (when present) a hint from the patient." },
        { term: "Affective gateway", def: "One of three emotionally charged regions through which patients share suicidal thought: depression, anxiety, and hopelessness; situational or interpersonal turmoil and anger; psychotic process. If one gate fails, try another." },
        { term: "Negative statement of inquiry", def: "A question phrased to invite no ('You haven't thought of killing yourself?'). It signals disapproval and the hassled interviewer's hope that nothing comes up; patients oblige." },
        { term: "CASE Approach", def: "Chronological Assessment of Suicide Events: Shea's flexible strategy for eliciting suicidal ideation, planning, behavior, and intent across four timeframes (presenting, recent, past, immediate), using validity techniques to maximize engagement and truthfulness." },
        { term: "Presenting suicide events", def: "CASE region 1: ideation, plans, or attempts in roughly the past 48 hours to several weeks. Explored first by building a verbal video of the attempt or plan." },
        { term: "Recent suicide events", def: "CASE region 2: the preceding 2 months. The richest source of reflected intent and the place a withheld method of choice usually hides. Explored with gentle assumption, verbal videos, denials of the specific, the catch-all question, and symptom amplification." },
        { term: "Past suicide events", def: "CASE region 3: earlier than 2 months ago. Limited to behaviors that could change today's triage: the most serious attempt, similarity of current triggers and state, rough count of attempts, and the most recent one." },
        { term: "Immediate suicide events", def: "CASE region 4, now and next: suicidal feelings and intent during the interview itself, likelihood of thoughts after leaving, hopelessness, reasons for living, and (here or at the close) safety planning." },
        { term: "Verbal video", def: "A step-by-step reconstruction of an attempt or plan built with fact-finding and sequencing behavioral incidents, so the clinician can picture it unfolding. Borrowed from behavioral chain analysis in CBT and DBT." },
        { term: "Nixon gap", def: "A skipped stretch in the patient's verbal video (for example, from taking pills straight to 'the ER'). The clinician rewinds to where the gap began and fills it with behavioral incidents." },
        { term: "Method-in-hand question", def: "'Have you ever gotten the [method] out while thinking of killing yourself?' The first of the four-step kick-start to a verbal video, followed by when, where, and a summarizing invitation." },
        { term: "Summarizing invitation", def: "A brief recap of the scene followed by an invitation to walk through what happened step by step; the fourth step that launches a verbal video." },
        { term: "Bridging question", def: "The question that carries the CASE Approach from one timeframe to the next, usually by asking about the same method over the stated period: 'Over the past 2 months, have you had any other thoughts of overdosing?'" },
        { term: "Cannon question", def: "Several denials of the specific fired as one question. Patients answer only part of it, usually the last item, and need to lie only once. Ask each separately and wait." },
        { term: "Lethal points", def: "The concrete facts that reveal seriousness of intent: loaded, safety off, how long held, pills left in the bottle, who was told, whether rescue was possible, what stopped the attempt." },
        { term: "Method of choice", def: "The way the patient most intends to die. Dangerous patients may describe a lesser method and withhold this one, fearing it will be removed; it often emerges only through gentle assumption, denial of the specific, or the catch-all question." },
        { term: "Safety planning", def: "A collaborative list of steps the patient will take if suicidal thoughts return, from self-soothing to calling a crisis line or returning to the hospital. Generally preferred to contracting as a deterrent." },
        { term: "Safety contracting", def: "Asking the patient to promise to contact the clinician before acting on any suicidal thought. Weak as a deterrent, never a guarantee, avoided with borderline or passive-aggressive pathology, but selectively valuable as an assessment tool." },
        { term: "Interpersonal push", def: "The gentle pressure created by an earnest, eye-to-eye request to promise safety, which can prompt nonverbal leakage of hidden ambivalence or intent." },
        { term: "Reasons for living", def: "What keeps the patient alive: children, faith, duties, hope. Explored directly ('What things in your life make you want to go on living?') and often surfacing spontaneously during a verbal video." }
      ]
    },
    /* ───────────────────────── CHAPTER 18 ───────────────────────── */
    {
      id: "ch18", num: 18, title: "Exploring Violent and Homicidal Ideation", sub: "From domestic violence to mass murder",
      summary: "A homicide assessment has the same bones as a suicide assessment: risk factors, a tetrad of lethality, and a chronological interview, here the CADE Approach, that uses validity techniques to uncover what the patient has actually fantasized, planned, armed for, and done. Past violence is the strongest predictor. Never rely on the patient's word alone; victims, family, police, and records often know more. And keep yourself safe while you ask.",
      concepts: [
        { h: "Three domains of violence", b: "Meloy's affective violence (impulsive, angry, often intoxicated: the realm of most domestic violence) and predatory violence (planned, often pleasurable, little remorse: rapists, sadists, psychopathy). Shea adds biologically induced violence from pathophysiology (schizophrenia, dysphoric mania, LSD, PCP, bath salts) or structural damage (tumor, seizures, trauma, dementia). Naming the domains helps you remember when to ask and where your blind spots are." },
        { h: "Structured tools predict months, not days", b: "The HCR-20, COVR, VRAG, and PCL-R are actuarial instruments. They have not been shown to predict immediate (24 to 48 hours) or imminent (up to 2 weeks) violence, and reading from them costs you eye contact and flow. They do predict violence over months (the COVR is validated for 20 weeks), so learn them for ongoing or forensic work; the initial interview relies on the clinician." },
        { h: "Risk factors for violence", b: "A history of violence is the single strongest indicator; add other signs of poor impulse control (property destruction, arrests, reckless driving or spending, sexual acting out, suicide attempts). Young men, poverty, and growing up abused or watching abuse raise risk. Alcohol and drug use disorders are the most important diagnoses (10 to 16 times the risk), then antisocial and borderline personality, organic conditions, psychosis, and mania. Most psychotic patients, most of the time, are safe." },
        { h: "Psychopathy and intermittent explosive disorder", b: "Psychopathy is a small, far more dangerous subset of antisocial personality: almost no superego, enjoyment of violence including toward animals. It is Meloy's predatory domain. Intermittent explosive disorder is over-applied; it cannot be diagnosed when another disorder, alcohol, drugs, or antisocial personality explains the violence." },
        { h: "The tetrad of lethality for violence", b: "A recent serious violent act, often with the patient brought by police; psychotic process of a kind that precipitates violence; indication from the interview itself of intent; and a lying patient with corroborative evidence of intended violence. After affective violence, lingering anger argues for a cool-down admission. A predator's calm is a mask: look through it. Beware powder-keg environments where the former victim, other impulsive people, or alcohol are present." },
        { h: "Dangerous psychotic process, plus paranoia", b: "The same three as in suicide (command hallucinations, alien control, hyper-religiosity, especially missions from God to rid the earth of some evil) and a fourth: paranoid process, common in schizophrenia but also in dysphoric mania and psychotic depression. Finding the delusion is not enough; find out what the patient intends to do about it and whether they have armed themselves." },
        { h: "Knoll and Meloy's four steps", b: "A paranoid progression toward violence, usable with any paranoid patient and, adapted, with non-psychotic grievance: (1) a present danger or wrong is perceived; (2) no non-violent or legal remedy seems possible, so violence becomes defensive and good; (3) the act is fantasized and pictured; (4) resolve, planning, procurement, practice, a date. Each step has its own questions. Consult a colleague, protect yourself, and consider a Tarasoff warning." },
        { h: "Don't rely on the patient", b: "Family, friends, victims, police, prior clinicians, the EHR (arrests, commitments, restraining orders), court records, and social media often hold the critical pieces. Domestic violence perpetrators minimize; victims usually give the better history. Document everything and call the receiving clinician: commitments get reversed at the door when information isn't passed on." },
        { h: "Domestic violence caveats", b: "Abuse is about power and control, and the assessment itself can provoke new violence. Consult first, safety-plan with the victim before interviewing the perpetrator, never ask the batterer in front of the victim, and never reveal what the victim told you; use police, neighbors, courts, and medical records instead. Snider's five emergency questions for a female victim: increasing frequency or severity, weapon use or threat, belief he could kill her, beaten while pregnant, violent constant jealousy." },
        { h: "The CADE Approach", b: "Set the stage through one of many gates (psychosis, conflict, money, revenge, jealousy, politics, gangs, terrorism, pleasure or sexual desire). Screen everyone ('Have you ever lost your temper?'). Then presenting, recent (2 months), past, and immediate violent events, in that order, with verbal videos, behavioral incidents that cut through minimization, gentle assumption, denial of the specific, the catch-all, and symptom amplification. Reflected intent beats stated intent." }
      ],
      pearls: [
        "Put your money on past violence. If you want one predictor, that is it; recent violence against the same person is stronger still.",
        "Before asking a suspected batterer about his own violence, ask whether his partner has ever lost control and hit him. Feeling equally heard makes the next answer more honest. Then: 'How often have you struck or grabbed her, if at all?'",
        "Replace opinion questions ('Did you hurt her badly?') with behavioral ones: fist, open hand, or weapon? Bruises? Stitches, how many? X-rays? Broken bones?",
        "Amplify to beat minimization: 'How many times have you hit her, 20, 40, more?'",
        "With a paranoid patient, after the delusion: 'What have you thought about doing to protect yourself?' and 'Have you armed yourself at home?'",
        "Get enough to identify a potential victim (workplace, neighborhood, routine) early and naturally; ask for the last name after the assessment so you don't spook the patient.",
        "Ask about thoughts and feelings before, during, and after past violence. Thoughts that trouble a patient are reassuring; thoughts that arouse are not. 'If the price were right, is there anything you would not do?'",
        "Safety contracts are worthless as deterrents to violence and make you look naive to a predator. Say instead that you would be foolish to trust him and don't, and ask him how dangerous he thinks he is right now.",
        "Any patient with a history of violence of any kind should be screened for sexual violence; incest, rape, and pedophilia often occur without any other violence.",
        "If it crosses your mind that you may be in danger, leave the room. Irritability, boisterousness, threats, attacks on objects, and confusion predict immediate assault; check for intoxication and organic signs."
      ],
      practice: [],
      quiz: [
        { q: "Which single factor is the most robust statistical predictor of future violence?", opts: ["A diagnosis of schizophrenia", "A history of previous violence", "Male sex", "Low income"], a: 1, w: "Past violence is the best bet, with recent violence against the intended victim stronger still. Schizophrenia alone does not make a patient dangerous; specific active psychotic processes do." },
        { q: "Meloy's predatory violence is best described as:", opts: ["Impulsive and fueled by anger, often intoxicated", "Planned, often pleasurable, with little remorse", "Caused directly by brain pathology", "Limited to domestic settings"], a: 1, w: "Affective violence is the hot, impulsive kind. Predatory violence is cold and premeditated, the domain of psychopathy. Shea adds a third domain, biologically induced violence." },
        { q: "Structured instruments such as the HCR-20 and COVR are best used for:", opts: ["Predicting violence within the next 24 to 48 hours", "Replacing the clinical interview in the emergency department", "Predicting violence over the following months in ongoing or forensic work", "Deciding whether to call the police tonight"], a: 2, w: "They have no demonstrated power for immediate or imminent risk and disrupt engagement in an initial interview, but they predict violence over months (COVR validated for 20 weeks)." },
        { q: "In the tetrad of lethality for violence, what distinguishes the affective from the predatory patient after a recent violent act?", opts: ["Predators are always intoxicated", "Lingering anger in an affective patient argues for a cool-down admission; a predator's calm is a mask to look through", "Affective patients never need admission", "There is no difference"], a: 1, w: "Simmering anger after affective violence is a warning. The calm of a predator before or after violence means nothing and can lull an unwary clinician." },
        { q: "Which is the extra psychotic risk factor Shea stresses for violence beyond the three shared with suicide?", opts: ["Thought blocking", "Paranoid process", "Negative symptoms", "Alexithymia"], a: 1, w: "Command hallucinations, alien control, and hyper-religiosity carry over from suicide assessment. Paranoia, seen in schizophrenia, dysphoric mania, and psychotic depression, is the additional one; ask what the patient intends to do about the persecutors." },
        { q: "In Knoll and Meloy's progression, which step marks the shift at which violence comes to feel defensive and morally justified?", opts: ["Perceiving a present danger", "Concluding there is no non-violent or legal way to stop the persecutors", "Fantasizing the act", "Picking a date"], a: 1, w: "Step two: once no other remedy seems possible, violence becomes necessary and good within the psychotic worldview. Ask what has been tried, what avenues exist, how certain and how soon." },
        { q: "You suspect a patient of intimate partner violence. According to Shea, which approach is most likely to yield a valid answer?", opts: ["Ask him directly whether he has ever hit his partner", "Ask in front of his partner so she can correct him", "Have him describe his worst argument, ask whether she has ever hit him, then ask how often he has struck or grabbed her, if at all", "Tell him what she reported and ask him to respond"], a: 2, w: "Asking first about the partner's violence makes the patient feel heard and safer; the gentle assumption that follows is more likely to be answered truthfully. Never ask in front of the victim or reveal what the victim said." },
        { q: "Which of the following was among Snider's five most predictive emergency department questions for female victims of IPV?", opts: ["Does he drink?", "Has he ever used or threatened you with a weapon?", "Does he have a job?", "Were his parents violent?"], a: 1, w: "The five: increasing frequency or severity, weapon use or threat, belief that he is capable of killing her, beaten while pregnant, violent and constant jealousy." },
        { q: "During the verbal video of a presenting violent act, which question best cuts through minimization?", opts: ["Did you hurt her badly?", "Did she need stitches, and how many?", "Do you feel bad about it?", "Why did you do it?"], a: 1, w: "Behavioral incidents replace opinion questions: fist or open hand, bruises, emergency visit, stitches, X-rays, broken bones. Symptom amplification helps with frequency." },
        { q: "What is Shea's view of safety contracting with a predatory or antisocial patient?", opts: ["Essential before discharge", "Useful as a deterrent", "Worthless and liable to make the clinician look naive; better to state candidly that trust must be earned and ask how dangerous he thinks he is now", "Effective if written and signed"], a: 2, w: "Predators are professional liars with no guilt; contracts have no deterrent value even for affective violence, since the next angry exchange resets everything. As an assessment tool they help only with patients truly troubled by their violence." }
      ],
      glossary: [
        { term: "Affective violence", def: "Meloy: impulsive violence driven by intense anger and emotion, usually in response to interpersonal stress and often under alcohol or drugs. The realm of most domestic, workplace, school, and street violence." },
        { term: "Predatory violence", def: "Meloy: violence that is planned in advance, often pleasurable to the perpetrator, and followed by little or no remorse. The domain of many rapists, sadistic abusers, and people with psychopathy." },
        { term: "Biologically induced violence", def: "Shea's third domain: violence caused directly by brain pathophysiology (schizophrenia, dysphoric mania, LSD, PCP, bath salts) or structural pathology (tumor, partial complex seizures, infection, trauma, multiple sclerosis, dementia)." },
        { term: "Actuarial approach", def: "Risk assessment by structured gathering of statistical risk factors (HCR-20, COVR, VRAG), as opposed to clinical judgment. Validated for prediction over months, not for immediate or imminent violence." },
        { term: "HCR-20", def: "Historical Clinical Risk Management-20: a structured violence risk instrument with historical, clinical, and risk-management items (young age at first violence, relationship instability, employment problems, early maladjustment, supervision failure, lack of insight, negative attitudes, exposure to destabilizers, lack of support, noncompliance, current stress)." },
        { term: "COVR", def: "Classification of Violence Risk: risk assessment software validated for predicting violence over the 20 weeks after completion." },
        { term: "Immediate / imminent / short-term risk", def: "Timeframes for violence prediction: immediate (24 to 48 hours after the interview), imminent (up to 2 weeks), short-term (up to 6 months), long-term (beyond). This chapter targets the first two." },
        { term: "Psychopathy", def: "A small, much more dangerous subset of antisocial personality disorder: near-absent superego function and marked enjoyment of violence, including toward animals. Meloy's predatory violence; measured by Hare's PCL-R." },
        { term: "Intermittent explosive disorder", def: "Recurrent violent outbursts with a 2.7% one-year prevalence; diagnosable only when no other psychiatric disorder, alcohol, drugs, or antisocial personality explains the violence, so usually inappropriate in domestic violence." },
        { term: "Tetrad of lethality (violence)", def: "Four presentations suggesting near-term violence: a recent serious violent act; psychotic process of a kind that precipitates violence; intent indicated in the interview itself; a lying patient with corroborative evidence of intended violence." },
        { term: "Powder keg environment", def: "A setting that raises the risk of violence: the former victim present, companions with poor impulse control, or alcohol and drugs in the patient or those around them. Hunt for it and use corroborative interviews." },
        { term: "Paranoid process", def: "Persecutory beliefs seen in schizophrenia, schizoaffective disorder, dysphoric mania, and psychotic depression. For violence assessment, the key question is not whether the delusion exists but what the patient intends to do about it and whether they have armed themselves." },
        { term: "Mission from God", def: "A hyper-religious psychotic theme in which the patient believes God wants some evil removed from the earth; an intense preoccupation with right and wrong that may signal homicidal ideation." },
        { term: "Knoll and Meloy's four steps", def: "The cognitive progression of paranoid violence: perceive a present danger; conclude no non-violent or legal remedy exists so violence is defensive and good; fantasize and picture the act; resolve to act with planning, procurement, practice, and a date. Adaptable to non-psychotic grievance." },
        { term: "Suicide by cop", def: "A psychotic or desperate person provoking police into using lethal force; one of several ways psychotic patients themselves are at highest risk of harm." },
        { term: "Tarasoff duty", def: "The legal obligation, from a United States court decision, to warn or protect an identifiable potential victim whom the clinician believes to be in danger, even at the cost of confidentiality." },
        { term: "Intimate partner violence", def: "IPV: threatened, attempted, or completed physical or sexual assault by a partner, driven by the perpetrator's need for power and control. Partner rape is a common component." },
        { term: "Snider's five questions", def: "Emergency department questions for female IPV victims that best predicted further violence: increasing frequency or severity in 6 months; weapon used or threatened; belief he is capable of killing her; beaten while pregnant; violent and constant jealousy." },
        { term: "Homicidal gates", def: "Entry points for raising violence, more numerous than the three suicide gates: psychosis, interpersonal conflict, money and practical needs, revenge, jealousy, politics, gangs and organized crime, terrorism, pleasure and pathological sexual desire." },
        { term: "CADE Approach", def: "Chronological Assessment of Dangerous Events: the CASE Approach adapted to violent and homicidal ideation. Set the stage, then explore presenting, recent (2 months), past, and immediate violent events with the same validity techniques." },
        { term: "Worst-argument technique", def: "Setting the stage for IPV questions by having the patient describe the worst argument with a partner in detail, asking whether the partner ever lost control and hit them, then asking by gentle assumption how often the patient has struck or grabbed the partner, if at all." },
        { term: "Minimization", def: "The routine downplaying of violence by perpetrators. Countered with behavioral incidents about concrete injuries (bruises, stitches, X-rays), symptom amplification for frequency, and corroborative sources." },
        { term: "Superego window", def: "Meloy's suggestion to explore thoughts and feelings before, during, and after past violent acts to gauge guilt and shame; violent thoughts that trouble a patient are less worrying than thoughts that arouse." },
        { term: "Price question", def: "'If the price were right, is there anything you would not do?' With 'Is there anything you have done that made you feel guilty?', a probe for sociopathy and predatory process." },
        { term: "Stalking", def: "Repeatedly following or monitoring a person; illegal. Explored with behavioral incidents (where, how often, windows, cameras, weapons carried) and symptom amplification, while naturally gathering enough to identify the person if a warning is needed." },
        { term: "Predictors of immediate assault", def: "Common-sense signs that a patient may become violent in the interview or on release: irritability, boisterousness, verbal and physical threats, attacks on objects, confusion, and signs of intoxication or organic illness." }
      ]
    }
  ];
})();
